# ~/.agentlock/guard.conf — which commands need which lock. One rule per line:
# <resource><TAB><extended regex matched against the Bash command>
# A rule for "sim" is satisfied by a lock named "sim" or any "sim:<name>".
# Copy this file to ~/.agentlock/guard.conf, keep only what applies to the machine,
# and run `agentlock check-conf` after editing: a rule that is not a valid regex
# makes the guard block every command until it is fixed.
# Before matching, continued lines are joined, heredoc bodies are blanked and each quoted
# string is cut to its first word, so `open -a "Simulator"` still matches and a quoted
# sentence does not; a $(…) or backtick substitution, quoted or not, is matched as a
# statement of its own, so `udid="$(xcrun simctl boot …)"` needs its lock. Rules are then matched per statement (split on ; || & and newlines)
# against the segments that run something: a segment whose command only reads or prints text
# (grep, echo, cat, sed …) is not matched unless it holds a $(…) or a shell reads its output.
# Separators are removed, so a rule must not depend on seeing | ; or & around a command.
# `agentlock acquire X … && <command>` counts as holding X for the rest of that statement.

# Anything that takes the screen, keyboard focus or the simulator window.
ui	\b(screencapture|cliclick)\b|\bosascript(\s+(-e|-l|\S+\.(scpt|applescript))|\s*<<)|(^|[;|&({`]|\b(if|then|else|do|env|nohup|time|exec|caffeinate|sudo|xargs)\s+)\s*osascript\b|open -a "?'?Simulator|open -b com\.apple\.dt\.Devices|DeviceHub|xcodebuild[^|;&]*platform=macOS[^|;&]*\s(test|test-without-building)(\s|$)|xcodebuild[^|;&]*\s(test|test-without-building)\s[^|;&]*platform=macOS
# iOS simulators (the verbs that change or read a device; `list`, `bootstatus` and friends need none), and a test
# run on a simulator-family destination (platform=iOS/tvOS/watchOS/visionOS, a destination starting with id=/name=/OS=, or no
# destination at all, which an iOS scheme resolves to a simulator; uppercase BUILD_SETTING=value pairs do not
# count as a destination; a macOS-only project edits this, since the rule cannot know the scheme): it serialises
# with other simulator users, not with the screen. Each keyed form is written twice, for the action before and
# after the destination (`xcodebuild test -destination …` and `xcodebuild -destination … test`)
# (0.4.7; before, it took ui). A macOS test run (platform=macOS) drives a window: ui above, and not sim.
sim	\bsimctl\s+(boot|shutdown|install|launch|terminate|io|erase|openurl)\b|xcodebuild[^|;&]*(platform=(iOS|tvOS|watchOS|visionOS|xrOS)|-destination[[:space:]]+['"]?(id|name|OS)=)[^|;&]*\s(test|test-without-building)(\s|$)|xcodebuild[^|;&]*\s(test|test-without-building)\s[^|;&]*(platform=(iOS|tvOS|watchOS|visionOS|xrOS)|-destination[[:space:]]+['"]?(id|name|OS)=)|xcodebuild([^|;&=]|[A-Z_][A-Z0-9_]*=[^[:space:]|;&]*)*\s(test|test-without-building)(\s([^|;&=]|[A-Z_][A-Z0-9_]*=[^[:space:]|;&]*)*)?(\||;|&|$)

# Physical devices, and a test run on one (platform=iOS followed by a comma, not "Simulator"), which takes device as well as sim.
# devicectl's reads (`list …`, `device info …`) need no lock; its acting verbs are listed (Xcode 27.2, 2026-11: a new verb
# that acts goes on this list, in the project's copy), with global options before the verb allowed, each with at most one
# value (`-j out.json`, `--timeout 5`, `-j out\ file.json`; inside brackets whitespace is `[:space:]`, since `\s` there is two
# literal characters). Not covered: a bare `-` as a value, a quoted verb (`'device' install`), as for every verb-listed rule.
# A read before an `acquire && …` chain once blocked a whole command.
device	\bdevicectl(\s+-\S+(\s+[^-[:space:]]\S*(\\ \S*)*)?)*\s+(device\s+(install|uninstall|process|copy|reboot|notify|disconnect|lock|unlock)|manage)\b|\b(ios-deploy|idevice[a-z]*)\b|\bxctrace\s+(record|export)\b|\bxcodebuild[^|;&]*platform=(iOS|tvOS|watchOS|visionOS),[^|;&]*\s(test|test-without-building)(\s|$)|\bxcodebuild[^|;&]*\s(test|test-without-building)\s[^|;&]*platform=(iOS|tvOS|watchOS|visionOS),
# Web projects: a dev server on a fixed port, a shared dev database.
# port:3000	\b(npm run dev|next dev|vite)\b
# db:dev	\b(prisma migrate|rails db:|alembic upgrade)\b

# Heavy work. Needs no lock of its own, but is blocked for everyone else while
# some session holds "quiet" for a measurement.
heavy	\b(xcodebuild|swift build|swift test|npm run build|npm test|pnpm (build|test)|cargo (build|test)|playwright|docker build|ffmpeg)\b
